# nanoSoC FPGA Toolkit - what never belongs in this repository
#
# TWO JOBS, and the second is the one that matters. The first is the ordinary
# one: keep build output out. The second is that the vendor-collateral scanner
# (ci/check-vendor-collateral.sh) scans the UNTRACKED corpus as well as the
# tracked one - that is the half which catches the file that sat in a working
# tree for three weeks and then went in under `git add -A`. That scan is only
# meaningful if genuinely ignorable output is genuinely ignored; without this
# file every stray log in a checkout is reported and the report stops being
# read. So an entry here is a claim that a path is NOISE, and anything that
# might be vendor collateral must NOT be listed - being ignored here is exactly
# how it would reach a commit unseen.
#
# Copyright (C) 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)

# --- run output -------------------------------------------------------------
build/
*.jou
*.log
*.str
.Xil/
.srcs/
.cache/
.hbs/
.runs/
vivado_pid*.str
webtalk*.jou
webtalk*.log
hs_err_pid*.log

# --- python -----------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# --- editor / OS debris -----------------------------------------------------
*~
*.swp
*.bak
*.orig
.DS_Store

# --- deliberately NOT ignored ------------------------------------------------
# *.bit *.bin *.dcp *.xsa *.hwh *.ltx *.xci *.xcix *.edn *.edf
#   Every one of these is something the scanner exists to catch. Ignoring them
#   would hide them from the untracked scan, which is the opposite of the point.
#   If one is in your tree it should be reported, every time, until it is gone.
